WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Roles are useful because people can understand them.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:13.720
Developer, analyst, billing administrator, support agent, project owner, and tenant administrator are easier to reason about than hundreds of raw checks.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.180
Permissions are useful because software needs precision.

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:29.120
A mature pattern maps roles to specific permissions such as read report, update project, approve request, export invoice, manage users, or rotate integration secrets.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.680
Overbroad administrator roles are risky because they grant more access than users or services usually need.

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:43.500
It may be convenient to assign full administrator access during development or support, but broad standing privilege increases the impact of mistakes and compromise.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:52.960
Scoped roles, temporary access, just-in-time concepts, and explicit administrative permissions can reduce exposure while still supporting operations.

8
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.200
Role explosion is the opposite failure mode.

9
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.940
If every exception becomes a new role, the model becomes impossible to review.

10
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.800
Permissions should have clear names, clear owners, and clear reasons to exist.

11
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.660
Roles should be understandable to humans but precise enough for code.

12
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:19.800
Environment-specific roles should be handled carefully so development, staging, and production access do not drift into unsafe patterns.

13
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.680
Access review keeps role and permission models healthy.

14
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:31.300
Users change jobs, services change responsibilities, projects end, and temporary exceptions become stale.

15
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:42.600
Reviews should ask whether roles still match business responsibilities, whether permissions are too broad, whether administrative access is justified, and whether service accounts have only the access they need.

16
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.620
Good role design is not static; it is maintained through policy, review, and evidence.
